# ================================================================== PART 1 · THE MECHANICS
flow(id="money", part=0, scope="world",
 title="What Money Is, and Who Makes It",
 kick="Most money is not printed by a government. It is typed into existence by a bank each time it makes a loan.",
 sum="Finance is the business of making, lending and moving money, and of charging for each of those things. It is described in a vocabulary that most people were never taught, so decisions that set the cost of a home, the size of a wage and the budget of a school are made in words that sound like they belong to someone else. This map explains those words one at a time and then shows who uses them. The first four chapters are the mechanics. They name no culprits. They set out how the parts move, so that every later chapter can be followed without stopping.<br><br>The first fact is the one that surprises people most. A government mint does not make most of the money in a country. Banks do. When a bank approves a loan it does not hand over money that savers left with it. It writes a new number into the borrower's account, and that number is new money. Whoever is allowed to do this decides who gets money first and what it is used for. From there the map runs from the top of the system down to the household.",
 bridge="The four slides build one idea in order. The first says what money is. The second shows a bank creating it. The third sets out what limits a bank and what does not. The fourth maps how much bank-made money every country runs on.",
 out="Money is a promise, most of it is created by private banks when they lend, and the amount varies enormously between countries. The next chapter adds the price that is charged for it.",
 rec="Most money is created by private banks at the moment they make a loan, and it disappears when the loan is repaid.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "Bank of England, Money in the Modern Economy: An Introduction (Quarterly Bulletin 2014 Q1)", "u": BOE_INTRO},
      {"t": "Bank of England, Money Creation in the Modern Economy (2014), summarised by Positive Money", "u": BOE_CREATE},
      {"t": "World Bank, Domestic credit to private sector (per cent of GDP)", "u": WBDATA + "FS.AST.PRVT.GD.ZS"}],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Money Is a Promise Somebody Wrote Down",
   "Start with what is in your pocket and in your account, because they are different things. The Bank of England, the central bank of the United Kingdom, set this out for the public in 2014. It describes three kinds of money, and it calls each one an IOU, which is a written promise to pay. Notes and coins are a promise from the central bank. The balance in your bank account is a promise from your bank to you. The third kind is central bank reserves, which are balances that banks hold with the central bank and use to pay each other. You never touch those. The Bank of England then gives the proportions: 97 per cent of the money held by the public is bank deposits, and only about 3 per cent is notes and coins. So when people say money, they nearly always mean a number in a private bank's computer. That number is the bank's promise to pay. The next slide shows where the number comes from.",
   BOE_INTRO, w=1, ex=["boe2014"], pl=["boe"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because it explains an idea that applies everywhere. The ringed circle is the Bank of England, whose paper it draws on."),
  st("2014", "London", LONDON, 3.4, "A Bank Loan Creates New Money",
   "Most people picture a bank as a storehouse. Savers put money in, and the bank lends that same money out. The Bank of England says this picture is wrong. In its words, whenever a bank makes a loan, it at once creates a matching deposit in the borrower's account, and so creates new money. Follow one loan. A couple is approved for a mortgage of 200,000. The bank does not take 200,000 from anyone. It types 200,000 into the couple's account and records that they owe it 200,000 plus interest. A moment earlier that money did not exist. The couple pay the seller, the seller's bank balance rises, and the new money is now moving around the economy. This gives the first rules of the system. If banks lend more, there is more money. If borrowers repay faster than banks lend, money disappears, because a repaid loan is a deleted number. And the interest owed is on top of the amount that was created.",
   BOE_CREATE, add={"United Kingdom": 0}, pl=["boe"], ex=["boe2014"]),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "What Limits a Bank, and What Does Not",
   "If a bank can create money by typing, what stops it? The Bank of England lists the limits. A bank needs a borrower who wants the loan and looks able to repay. It has to stay profitable, so it will not lend at a loss. It must keep a small cushion of its own money against losses, which a later chapter measures. And the central bank sets the interest rate that sits under every loan, which the next chapter explains. Notice what is missing from the list. Nothing requires a bank to lend for things a country needs. A bank lends where the loan is safest and most profitable for the bank. A loan secured on a house can be recovered by taking the house. A loan to a new workshop cannot be recovered so easily. So the private choice of what to lend against decides where a country's new money goes first. That is a public matter decided by private firms, and it is the starting point for everything else on this map.",
   BOE_CREATE, w=1, note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. It sets out limits that the Bank of England describes for banks in general."),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "How Much Bank-Made Money Each Country Runs On",
   "The World Bank measures the result country by country. Its figure is called domestic credit to the private sector. It adds up what households and businesses owe to banks and other lenders and compares it with the size of the economy, which is called GDP, the value of everything a country produces in a year. The map shades %(credit_n)s countries. In the United States the figure is about 201 per cent, in China about 194 per cent and in Japan about 189 per cent. In those countries people and firms owe roughly two years of the whole country's output. In %(credit_low)s countries the figure is under 30 per cent. Two things follow. In the heavily shaded countries the economy runs on debt to lenders, and a share of nearly every wage and every sale goes to them as interest. In the lightly shaded countries a household or small firm can hardly borrow at all, or borrows from an informal lender at rates that a later slide maps. Neither end is comfortable for the borrower.",
   WBDATA + "FS.AST.PRVT.GD.ZS", data={"k": "credit", "only": None}, pl=["worldbank"]),
 ])

flow(id="interest", part=0, scope="world",
 title="Interest: The Price of Money",
 kick="Interest is the rent paid on borrowed money. One rate, set by a committee, sits underneath all the others.",
 sum="The last chapter showed that money is created as a loan. A loan has a price, and the price is interest. Interest is what turns the power to create money into an income. If a bank types 200,000 into an account and is repaid 380,000 over twenty-five years, the difference is what the bank earned for the typing and the risk.<br><br>Interest is also the main control in the whole system. When it goes up, borrowing slows, firms hire fewer people and prices rise more slowly. When it goes down, the reverse happens. A person who understands that chain can read almost any financial headline. This chapter lays the chain out link by link, then introduces the bond, which is the form in which governments and large companies borrow. It ends with a map of what a loan costs in each country, because the same money does not cost the same everywhere.",
 bridge="The first slide shows what interest does to a debt over time. The second follows a change in the central bank's rate through the economy, one step at a time. The third explains a bond and why its price moves the opposite way to interest rates. The fourth maps the price of a bank loan in every country that reports one.",
 out="Interest makes a debt grow, the central bank's rate moves every other rate, and a bond's price falls when rates rise. A loan costs many times more in some countries than in others. The next chapter sets out the other instruments.",
 rec="Interest is the price of money, one central rate moves all the others, and that price is several times higher in poorer countries.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "Bank of England, Money Creation in the Modern Economy (2014), summarised by Positive Money", "u": BOE_CREATE},
      {"t": "UN Trade and Development, A World of Debt 2025", "u": "https://unctad.org/publication/world-of-debt"},
      {"t": "World Bank, Lending interest rate (per cent)", "u": WBDATA + "FR.INR.LEND"}],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "Interest Is Rent on Money, and It Compounds",
   "Interest is a yearly charge for using someone else's money, written as a percentage. Borrow 1,000 at 10 per cent and the charge is 100 a year. The part that matters most is what happens when the charge is not paid. It is added to the debt, and next year's interest is charged on the larger amount. This is called compound interest. The arithmetic is simple and worth doing once. At 5 per cent, an unpaid debt doubles in about fourteen years. At 10 per cent it doubles in about seven. At 20 per cent, a common rate on a credit card, it doubles in under four. The same sums run in favour of the lender, whose money grows at those speeds without further work. So the rate is not a detail. A few points of difference decide whether a debt can ever be cleared or only kept alive. Every later chapter is about who pays which rate, and who decided it.",
   BOE_CREATE, w=1, note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. The doubling times are arithmetic and apply to any currency."),
  st("Today", "Five central banks", WORLD, 1.15, "One Rate Sits Under All the Others",
   "Banks borrow too, from each other and from the central bank. The central bank sets the rate for that borrowing. The Bank of England describes this rate as the final limit on how much money banks create, because it feeds into the rate on every loan. Follow the chain when a central bank raises its rate. Banks pay more for money, so they charge more for mortgages, car loans and business loans. Then fewer people borrow, so less new money is created. Then people and firms spend less, firms sell less and hire fewer workers, and prices rise more slowly. Now run it the other way. If the central bank cuts its rate, loans get cheaper, borrowing and spending pick up, and the prices of houses and shares usually climb. Either way the first effect lands on people with debts and people looking for work. The five pins mark the central banks whose rates matter most to the rest of the world. Part 2 looks at who sits on their committees.",
   BOE_CREATE, w=1, pl=["fed", "ecb", "boj", "boe", "pboc"],
   note="Ringed circles: the central banks of the United States, the euro area, Japan, the United Kingdom and China, each at its headquarters. No country is shaded, because the chain described here works the same way in each."),
  st("2020 to 2025", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "A Bond Is a Loan That Can Be Sold",
   "Governments and big companies rarely borrow from one bank. They sell bonds. A bond is a loan cut into pieces that anyone can buy. The buyer hands over, say, 1,000 and receives a fixed payment each year plus the 1,000 back at the end. The yearly payment as a share of the price is called the yield. The useful rule is this: when interest rates rise, the price of existing bonds falls. Suppose you hold a bond that pays 30 a year. If new bonds start paying 50, nobody will give you 1,000 for yours, so its price drops until 30 a year looks as good as 50 does on a new one. The yield is also a country's interest bill. UN Trade and Development compared what governments pay on ten-year bonds between 2020 and 2025. The United States paid an average of 2.8 per cent. Asia and Oceania paid 5.5, Latin America 7.1 and Africa 9.8. On each billion borrowed, that is 28 million a year for one government and 98 million for another.",
   "https://www.avvenire.it/economia/pagine/nel-silenzio-il-debito-stritola-i-paesi-fragili-interessi-a-10-in-un-anno", w=1, pl=["unctad"], ex=["unctad_rates"],
   note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the source gives averages for regions and not for single countries. Part 6 maps government interest bills country by country."),
  st("Latest year", "Every country with a figure", WORLD, 1.15, "The Same Money Costs More in Poorer Countries",
   "Governments pay different rates, and so do ordinary borrowers. The World Bank collects the lending rate, which is the interest banks charge businesses and households on loans. The map shades the %(lend_n)s countries that have reported one since 2018. In %(lend_lo)s of them the rate is under 6 per cent. In %(lend_hi)s it is 16 per cent or more, the level at which a debt doubles in under five years if nothing is paid. The highest rates on the map are in Madagascar, Zimbabwe, Argentina, Brazil and Malawi. Set this beside the first chapter. A farmer in Malawi and a farmer in Switzerland can want the same loan for the same tractor. One pays about 37 per cent a year and the other about 3. The money is created the same way in both countries, by a bank typing a number. What differs is the price. A person who pays ten times as much for money has to work ten times as hard to gain the same ground.",
   WBDATA + "FR.INR.LEND", data={"k": "lend", "only": None}, pl=["worldbank"]),
 ])

flow(id="instruments", part=0, scope="world",
 title="Shares, Leverage, Bets and Runs",
 kick="Four more working parts: owning a slice of a company, borrowing to enlarge a bet, betting on a price, and the panic that empties a bank.",
 sum="Two instruments are now on the table: the loan and the bond. Both are debts. This chapter adds the rest of the kit. A share is ownership, which is a claim on whatever a company has left after paying everyone else. Leverage is the use of borrowed money to make a bet larger than the bettor could afford alone. A derivative is a contract whose value depends on some other price. A run is what happens when everyone asks a bank for their money on the same day.<br><br>None of these is complicated once it is seen in a worked example. Together they explain why finance can earn a great deal in good years and why its losses arrive suddenly and are large enough to be passed to the public. Each slide gives the rule as an if and a then.",
 bridge="The first slide separates the shareholder from the lender. The second works through leverage with small numbers and then gives the real ratio from 2008. The third measures the market in bets on prices. The fourth explains a run, and maps every country that has had a banking collapse since 1970.",
 out="A share is a claim on profit, leverage multiplies both gains and losses, bets on prices now carry face values many times the world's output, and %(crises_n)s countries have been through a banking collapse. The next chapter takes money across a border.",
 rec="Shares are claims on profit, leverage multiplies gains and losses alike, and banking collapses are a regular feature of the system.",
 roles=[],
 src=[{"t": "S&P Dow Jones Indices, S&P 500 buybacks, third quarter 2025", "u": "https://press.spglobal.com/2025-12-18-S-P-500-Q3-2025-Buybacks-Post-Modest-6-2-Gain-to-249-0-Billion-After-Declining-20-1-Amidst-Uncertainty-in-Q2-Q4-2025-Expenditures-Expected-to-Post-Similar-Growth,-As-2025-Anticipates-a-Record-1-Trillion"},
      {"t": "Financial Crisis Inquiry Commission, Conclusions (2011)", "u": "https://fcic.law.stanford.edu/report/conclusions"},
      {"t": "Bank for International Settlements, OTC derivatives statistics at end-June 2025", "u": "https://www.bis.org/publ/otc_hy2512.htm"},
      {"t": "L. Laeven and F. Valencia, Systemic Banking Crises Database: 1970-2025, IMF Working Paper 26/94", "u": "https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/WP"}],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "A Share Is a Slice of Ownership",
   "A company can raise money in two ways. It can borrow, by taking a loan or selling bonds. Or it can sell shares, which are slices of ownership. The two buyers stand in different places. The lender is promised a fixed payment and is paid first. The shareholder is promised nothing and is paid last, but everything left over belongs to the shareholders. That is why a share has no upper limit on what it can earn. Shareholders are paid in two ways. A dividend is cash handed out per share. A buyback is the company spending its cash to buy its own shares, which pushes the price up for those who still hold them. Shareholders also vote for the board that hires and fires the managers. So here is the rule to carry forward. If a company can lower its wage bill, its tax bill or its spending on the future, more is left over, and what is left over goes to the shareholders. Part 4 shows who the shareholders are.",
   "https://press.spglobal.com/2025-12-18-S-P-500-Q3-2025-Buybacks-Post-Modest-6-2-Gain-to-249-0-Billion-After-Declining-20-1-Amidst-Uncertainty-in-Q2-Q4-2025-Expenditures-Expected-to-Post-Similar-Growth,-As-2025-Anticipates-a-Record-1-Trillion",
   w=1, note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. Part 4 maps the size of each country's stock market."),
  st("2007", "New York", NY, 3.2, "Leverage: Borrowing to Make a Bet Bigger",
   "Leverage means buying with borrowed money. Take the smallest example. You have 10 of your own. You borrow 90 and buy something for 100. If its price rises by 10 per cent to 110, you repay the 90 and hold 20. Your own money has doubled. If its price falls by 10 per cent to 90, you repay the 90 and hold nothing. So leverage multiplies the gain, and it multiplies the loss by the same amount. A firm that borrows 90 for every 10 of its own is said to be leveraged ten to one. Now the real figure. The commission set up by the United States Congress to investigate the crash of 2008 found that the largest investment banks had been leveraged as high as 40 to 1. In its words, a fall of less than 3 per cent in the value of what they held could wipe out the firm. The gains from those bets had been paid out as profit and bonuses in the years before. Part 3 shows who took the loss.",
   "https://www.slate.com/id/2283607/pagenum/all/", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["fcic"], ex=["fcic2011"]),
  st("June 2025", "Basel", BASEL, 2.6, "A Derivative Is a Bet on a Price",
   "A derivative is a contract whose value depends on some other price: an interest rate, a currency, a barrel of oil. It began as protection. A farmer agrees in spring to sell the harvest at a fixed price in autumn, and no longer has to fear a fall. The same contract lets someone who grows nothing bet on that price, and most contracts today are between financial firms. The Bank for International Settlements counts the ones agreed privately between banks and their customers, called over-the-counter derivatives. At the end of June 2025 their face value was 846 trillion dollars. The whole world economy produces about 117 trillion dollars in a year. The face value overstates what is at risk, and the same bank puts the market value of the contracts at about 22 trillion. But the size shows where the effort of the largest banks goes. It also means that the firms are tied to each other by so many contracts that the failure of one threatens the rest.",
   "https://www.bis.org/publ/otc_hy2512.htm", add={"Switzerland": 0}, pl=["bis"], ex=["bis_otc2025"],
   note="Shaded: Switzerland, where the Bank for International Settlements, which publishes the count, has its headquarters. The contracts themselves are written in financial centres around the world."),
  st("1970 to 2025", "Every country", WORLD, 1.15, "A Run: Why Banks Collapse, and How Often",
   "One weakness is built into every bank. It owes its depositors their money on demand, but it has lent that money out for years. This works only while depositors do not all ask at once. If they do, the bank cannot pay, and a sound bank can fail in days. That is a run. Because a run can spread from bank to bank, governments insure deposits and central banks lend to banks in a panic. Keep that promise in mind, because the largest banks have learned to rely on it. Collapses are not rare. Two economists, Luc Laeven and Fabian Valencia, keep a database for the International Monetary Fund of every banking crisis severe enough to threaten a whole country's system. For 1970 to 2025 it lists %(crises_total)s episodes in %(crises_n)s countries, and %(crises_multi)s countries appear more than once. The map shades each country by its number of episodes. A country left in the palest tone has none in the table.",
   "https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/WP", data={"k": "crises", "only": None}, pl=["imf"], ex=["lv2026"]),
 ])

flow(id="borders", part=0, scope="world",
 title="Money Across Borders",
 kick="Every country has its own money, but one country's money sits in the middle of nearly every exchange.",
 sum="So far the mechanics have stayed inside one country. Trade, travel, migrant workers and investors all need to turn one money into another, and the price at which that happens is the exchange rate. Exchange rates decide what imported food and fuel cost, what a wage sent home is worth, and how heavy a foreign debt is.<br><br>The currencies do not meet as equals. One of them, the United States dollar, is on one side of nearly nine exchanges in ten, and is the money in which central banks keep more than half of their savings. That gives the committee that sets the dollar's interest rate an influence over countries whose people have no say in it. This chapter sets out the rules of exchange, measures the dollar's place, follows a change in the American rate around the world, and ends with the clearest historical case.",
 bridge="The first slide gives the if-then rules of an exchange rate. The second measures the dollar's position. The third follows a rise in the American interest rate out to a country that borrowed in dollars. The fourth is the example from 1982, when that sequence emptied the treasuries of a continent.",
 out="A falling currency raises the cost of imports and of foreign debt, the dollar is on one side of 89 per cent of currency trades, and a change in its interest rate can push other countries into crisis. That completes the mechanics. Part 2 names the bodies at the top.",
 rec="The dollar sits in the middle of the world's money, so a decision made for the United States sets conditions for everyone else.",
 roles=["Raised its interest rate", "Could no longer pay its dollar debts"],
 src=[{"t": "Bank for International Settlements, Triennial Central Bank Survey 2025", "u": "https://www.bis.org/press/p250930.htm"},
      {"t": "International Monetary Fund, Currency Composition of Official Foreign Exchange Reserves, data brief of 27 March 2026", "u": "https://data.imf.org/en/news/imf%20data%20brief%20march%2027"},
      {"t": "Federal Reserve History, Latin American Debt Crisis of the 1980s", "u": "https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/latin-american-debt-crisis"},
      {"t": "International Monetary Fund, Silent Revolution: The IMF 1979-1989, chapter 8", "u": "https://imf.org/external/pubs/Ft/history/2001/ch08.pdf"}],
 steps=[
  st("April 2025", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "An Exchange Rate Is the Price of One Money in Another",
   "An exchange rate is what one currency costs in another. It moves every day, because currencies are bought and sold like anything else. The rules that follow from a move are simple. If your country's currency falls, everything bought from abroad costs more: fuel, wheat, medicine, machines. Any debt owed in a foreign currency gets heavier, because more of your money is needed to buy each unit of theirs. Your exports get cheaper for foreigners, which helps only if you have something to export. If your currency rises, each of those runs the other way. So a fall in the currency is a pay cut for a whole country, and nobody votes on it. The trade that sets these prices is very large. The Bank for International Settlements surveys it every three years. In April 2025, currencies worth 9.6 trillion dollars changed hands on an average day. The world produces about 117 trillion dollars of goods and services in a year. Currency traders turn over that amount in about twelve days.",
   "https://www.bis.org/press/p250930.htm", w=1, pl=["bis"], ex=["bis_fx2025"], note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the survey figure is a world total."),
  st("2025", "Washington", WASH, 2.2, "One Currency Sits in the Middle",
   "Most exchanges do not go directly from one currency to another. They go through the dollar. The same survey found the dollar on one side of 89 per cent of all currency trades in April 2025. The euro was on one side of 29 per cent and the yen of 17 per cent. Central banks also keep savings in foreign money, called reserves, to pay for imports and defend their currency in an emergency. The International Monetary Fund counts them. At the end of 2025, about 57 per cent of the reserves it could identify were held in dollars and about 20 per cent in euros. Here is what that means in practice. Oil, grain and most foreign loans are priced in dollars. A country that needs any of them needs dollars first. It can earn them by exporting or borrow them. It cannot create them. Only one country can, and the interest rate on dollars is set by a committee in Washington whose legal duty is to the American economy.",
   "https://data.imf.org/en/news/imf%20data%20brief%20march%2027", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["fed", "imf"], ex=["bis_fx2025", "cofer2025"]),
  st("Today", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "When Washington Changes Its Rate, the World Follows",
   "Put the last chapters together and follow one decision. The American central bank raises its interest rate to slow prices at home. Dollars now earn more, so investors around the world sell other currencies and buy dollars. Those other currencies fall. A country that borrowed in dollars is hit twice: the interest on its loans goes up, and each dollar it owes costs more of its own money. Its central bank can raise its own rate to tempt investors back, which slows its economy and costs jobs. Or it can let the currency fall, which raises the price of imported food and fuel. Or the government can cut spending to find the dollars. Each of those choices is paid for by people who had no part in the decision. When the American rate is cut, the flow reverses. Cheap dollars pour into poorer countries as loans, and the debts are built that the next rise will make unpayable. The next slide is the time this happened to a whole continent.",
   "https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/latin-american-debt-crisis", w=1, pl=["fed"],
   note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country. It describes a sequence, and the next slide places it on the map."),
  st("1979 to 1989", "Washington and Mexico City", [-88, 28], 2.6, "Regional Example: The Rate Rise That Cost Latin America a Decade",
   "Regional Example: In the 1970s, banks in New York and London lent heavily to Latin American governments. The loans were in dollars, and the interest rate on them floated, which means it moved with American rates. In 1979 the American central bank, under its chairman Paul Volcker, began raising rates to stop inflation at home. The International Monetary Fund's own history records that short-term American rates went from 9.5 per cent in August 1979 to more than 16 per cent by May 1981. The interest bill on every floating loan rose with them. On 12 August 1982 Mexico's finance minister told Washington that Mexico could no longer pay. Sixteen Latin American countries ended up renegotiating their debts. The American central bank's own historians call the 1980s the region's lost decade. The banks were in danger too. The nine largest American banks had lent Latin America an amount equal to 176 per cent of their own capital. The rescue that followed was organised to keep those banks whole.",
   "https://www.federalreservehistory.org/essays/latin-american-debt-crisis", add={"United States": 0, "Mexico": 1}, arcs=[[WASH, MEXICO]], an=[["Washington", "Mexico City"]],
   pl=["fed", "imf"], ex=["mexico1982"], reg=True),
 ])

# ================================================================== PART 2 · THE TOP
flow(id="cbanks", part=1, scope="world",
 title="The Central Banks: Unelected, and at the Top",
 kick="The committees that set the price of money are appointed, hard to remove, and built to be out of reach of voters.",
 sum="The mechanics showed that one interest rate sits under all the others and that one currency sits in the middle of the rest. Someone sets that rate and issues that currency. In each country it is the central bank, and inside the central bank it is a committee of a dozen or so people. They are appointed for long terms and cannot easily be dismissed. They decide, several times a year, how expensive it will be to borrow and how hard it will be to find work.<br><br>This design is deliberate, and it is usually defended as protection against politicians who would print money to win elections. The same wall that keeps out politicians keeps out the public. The question for this chapter is who is inside the wall. It looks at who chooses the directors, how independent each central bank is, who gained when central banks created money on a vast scale, and who is given dollars when the world runs short.",
 bridge="The first slide shows the oddest feature of the American system: commercial banks hold shares in the regional central banks and elect most of their directors. The second maps how far each central bank stands from its government. The third shows who gained from the money created after 2008. The fourth shows which countries Washington supplied with dollars in 2020.",
 out="Central bankers are appointed and shielded from voters, private banks help choose the directors of the American reserve banks, the money created after 2008 raised the wealth of people who already held assets, and emergency dollars went to fourteen chosen central banks. The next chapter goes to the town where the rules for banks are written.",
 rec="The price of money is set by appointed committees that voters cannot remove, and their emergency measures favoured those who already owned assets.",
 roles=["Has a permanent dollar line with Washington", "Given a temporary dollar line in March 2020"],
 src=[{"t": "Federal Reserve Board, Overview: Federal Reserve System Boards of Directors", "u": "https://federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/directors/about.htm"},
      {"t": "Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, Role of Bankers on Federal Reserve's Boards of Directors", "u": "https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2022/feb/role-bankers-on-federal-reserves-boards-directors"},
      {"t": "D. Romelli, Central Bank Independence Extended index", "u": "https://cbidata.org"},
      {"t": "Bank of England, The Distributional Effects of Asset Purchases (12 July 2012)", "u": "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/news/2012/july/the-distributional-effects-of-asset-purchases-paper"},
      {"t": "Federal Reserve, press release of 19 March 2020 on temporary dollar arrangements", "u": "https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200319b.htm"}],
 steps=[
  st("Today", "The twelve American reserve banks", [-96, 39], 2.7, "Regional Example: Private Banks Elect the Directors of the Reserve Banks",
   "Regional Example: The American central bank is called the Federal Reserve. It has a board in Washington, whose members are named by the president, and twelve regional reserve banks that carry out much of its work. The regional banks have an unusual owner. By law, the commercial banks in each region that are members of the system hold stock in their reserve bank and are paid a dividend on it. The stock cannot be sold. Each reserve bank has nine directors. The member banks elect six of them. Three of those six are chosen to represent the banks and are usually bankers. The other three are meant to represent the public, but the banks choose them too. Only the last three are appointed by the board in Washington. Since 2010 the bankers' three may not vote on who becomes the reserve bank's president. The presidents of the reserve banks sit in turn on the committee that sets the American interest rate. So the firms that the central bank supervises help to choose the people who run it.",
   "https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2022/feb/role-bankers-on-federal-reserves-boards-directors", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["fed", "nyfed"], ex=["fed_directors"], reg=True,
   note="Shaded: the United States. Ringed circles: the Federal Reserve Board in Washington and the Federal Reserve Bank of New York, the largest of the twelve regional reserve banks."),
  st("2023", "Every country in the index", WORLD, 1.15, "How Far Each Central Bank Stands From Its Government",
   "The American arrangement is one version of a worldwide rule: keep the central bank at a distance from elected government. The economist Davide Romelli scores the law of each central bank on 42 points. They include who appoints the governor, how long the term is, whether the governor can be dismissed, and whether the bank may lend to the government. The result runs from 0 to 100, and a higher score means the law gives elected officials less control. The map shows %(cbi_n)s countries. The euro countries score 91, the highest group, because their central bank is fixed by a treaty that no single parliament can change. The United States scores 61. A high score is usually presented as good news. Read it also as a measure of distance from you. The more independent the bank, the less any vote can alter who sets the rate or what they aim at. The index does not measure the other distance, between the central bank and the commercial banks, which the last slide showed can be very short.",
   "https://cbidata.org", data={"k": "cbi", "only": None}, pl=["ecb"]),
  st("2009 to 2012", "London", LONDON, 3.2, "When Central Banks Created Money, Asset Owners Gained",
   "After the crash of 2008, central banks cut interest rates almost to zero. When that was not enough they did something new on a large scale. They created money and used it to buy bonds from investors. The practice is called quantitative easing. Buying bonds pushes their price up, and investors who sell them move into shares and property, which rise too. The Bank of England was asked by Parliament who had gained. It answered in July 2012. By then it had created 375 billion pounds. It estimated that this had raised the value of households' shares and bonds by about 600 billion pounds. Then it gave the distribution: the richest 5 per cent of households held 40 per cent of those assets. The Bank argued that everyone would have been worse off without the policy. That may be so. But the method chosen to rescue the economy worked by raising the price of things that the wealthy already owned, and that was a choice among possible methods.",
   "https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/news/2012/july/the-distributional-effects-of-asset-purchases-paper", add={"United Kingdom": 0}, pl=["boe"], ex=["boe_qe2012"],
   note="Shaded: the United Kingdom, the only country this source covers. Other large central banks ran the same kind of programme."),
  st("March 2020", "Washington and fourteen central banks", WORLD, 1.15, "In a Panic, the Dollars Go to a Chosen Few",
   "Chapter 4 showed that the world runs on dollars that only one country can create. In a panic everyone wants them at once. In March 2020, as the pandemic closed economies, the Federal Reserve used a tool called a swap line: it hands dollars to another central bank and takes that bank's currency as security. It has permanent, unlimited lines with five central banks: those of Canada, the United Kingdom, Japan, Switzerland and the euro area. On 19 March 2020 it added temporary lines for nine more: up to 60 billion dollars each for Australia, Brazil, South Korea, Mexico, Singapore and Sweden, and 30 billion each for Denmark, Norway and New Zealand. At the peak, in late May, 449 billion dollars was out on loan. Every other country was on its own. No treaty or vote decides who is on the list. The Federal Reserve decides. A government left off it has one place to go for dollars in an emergency, and that is the subject of a later chapter.",
   "https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20200319b.htm", w=1,
   add={"Canada": 0, "United Kingdom": 0, "Japan": 0, "Switzerland": 0, "Australia": 1, "Brazil": 1, "Korea, Rep.": 1, "Mexico": 1, "Singapore": 1, "Sweden": 1, "Denmark": 1, "Norway": 1, "New Zealand": 1},
   arcs=[[WASH, OTTAWA], [WASH, LONDON], [WASH, FRANKFURT], [WASH, BERN], [WASH, TOKYO], [WASH, MEXICO], [WASH, BRASILIA], [WASH, STOCKHOLM], [WASH, COPENHAGEN], [WASH, OSLO], [WASH, SEOUL], [WASH, SINGAPORE], [WASH, SYDNEY], [WASH, WELLINGTON]],
   an=[["Washington", "the fourteen central banks"]], pins=[[FRANKFURT[0], FRANKFURT[1], "European Central Bank"]], pl=["fed"], ex=["swap2020"],
   note="The European Central Bank is marked by a labelled point in Frankfurt and the euro countries are not shaded one by one. Lines to Asia and Oceania are drawn eastward across the Atlantic so that nothing is split at the edge of the map."),
 ])

flow(id="basel", part=1, scope="world",
 title="Basel: Where the Rules for Banks Are Written",
 kick="The world's rules on how much risk a bank may take are drafted by a committee of central bankers in a Swiss town.",
 sum="Central banks set the rate. A second question is how much risk a bank may take with the money it creates, and above all how thin its own cushion against losses may be. One might expect each parliament to decide that for its own banks. In practice the standard is written in Basel, in Switzerland, at the Bank for International Settlements, which is a bank owned by central banks.<br><br>A committee there, made up of central bankers and bank supervisors, agrees the standard. Governments then copy it into their own law. The process is technical and almost never debated in public. Its results decide how hard a banking crash will be and who will pay for it. This chapter sets out who sits in Basel, what the central number in the rulebook allows, and which banks the same bodies have declared too important to be allowed to fail.",
 bridge="The first slide introduces the bank that central banks own. The second shows the committee that writes the standard and its legal status. The third gives the key number, the cushion a bank must hold. The fourth maps the twenty-nine banks on the official list of those too large to fail.",
 out="A bank owned by 63 central banks hosts a committee from 28 jurisdictions that writes the bank rulebook. The rulebook lets a bank hold 3 of its own for every 100 it has at risk, and names 29 banks whose failure governments will not permit. The next chapter turns to the lender that governments themselves must use.",
 rec="The rulebook for banks is written by central bankers in Basel, and it lets a bank operate with a cushion of 3 per cent.",
 roles=["Home of banks on the list of 29"],
 src=[{"t": "Bank for International Settlements, member central banks", "u": "https://www.bis.org/about/organisation/members"},
      {"t": "Basel Committee on Banking Supervision, overview and membership", "u": "https://www.bis.org/bcbs"},
      {"t": "Basel Committee, Basel III leverage ratio framework", "u": "https://www.bis.org/publ/bcbs270.htm"},
      {"t": "Financial Stability Board, 2025 list of global systemically important banks", "u": "https://www.fsb.org/2025/11/2025-list-of-global-systemically-important-banks-g-sibs/"}],
 steps=[
  st("Since 1930", "Basel", BASEL, 2.6, "The Central Banks Have a Bank of Their Own",
   "The Bank for International Settlements was founded in 1930 and has its headquarters in Basel. It describes itself as a bank for central banks. It is owned by 63 of them, from countries that produce about 95 per cent of the world's output. Only central banks may hold its capital. It takes their deposits, manages part of their reserves, and gives their governors a regular place to meet. It also houses the committees that write the world's financial standards and publishes the counts of currency and derivatives trading used earlier on this map. No parliament elects anyone to it. Its importance for this story is as a meeting place. The people who set interest rates in different countries know each other, meet regularly in meetings that are not public, and reach common positions before those positions arrive in any national debate. The next slide is the most powerful of the committees that meet there.",
   "https://www.bis.org/about/organisation/members", add={"Switzerland": 0}, pl=["bis"], ex=["bis_members"]),
  st("Since 1974", "Basel", BASEL, 2.6, "A Committee Nobody Elected Writes the Rulebook",
   "The Basel Committee on Banking Supervision was set up in 1974. It calls itself the primary global standard setter for the regulation of banks. It has 45 members, all of them central banks or bank supervisors, from 28 jurisdictions. Its standards have no legal force. The Committee is not a world authority, and it says so. What happens instead is that the members agree a standard in Basel and then each takes it home, where the government writes it into national law. By then the terms have been settled among officials, after consultations in which the banks comment on the drafts. A national parliament is left to approve a text that its own central bank has already signed. Countries outside the 28 mostly adopt the same standard anyway, because banks and lenders expect it. So a rule that decides how safe your deposit is, and how much a bank can gamble, starts in a room where your country may not have a seat. The next slide gives the central number in that rule.",
   "https://www.bis.org/bcbs", add={"Switzerland": 0}, pl=["bcbs", "bis"], ex=["bcbs_members"]),
  st("Since 2014", "Basel", BASEL, 2.6, "Three of Its Own for Every Hundred at Risk",
   "A bank's capital is the money that belongs to its shareholders. It is the cushion that takes losses before depositors or taxpayers do. The mechanics chapter showed that a firm leveraged 40 to 1 is wiped out by a loss of under 3 per cent. After 2008 the Basel Committee wrote a floor under the cushion and called it the leverage ratio. A bank's core capital must be at least 3 per cent of everything it has at risk. Turn that around. For every 100 a bank lends or bets, 3 must be its own and 97 can be borrowed. That is leverage of about 33 to 1. A loss of 3 per cent across the bank's assets still empties the cushion. The Committee itself calls this a backstop, and the largest banks must hold somewhat more. But the plain reading is that the rule written after the worst crash in eighty years permits banks to run almost as thin as the firms that caused it. The public, standing behind the deposits, covers what the cushion cannot.",
   "https://www.bis.org/publ/bcbs270.htm", add={"Switzerland": 0}, pl=["bcbs"], ex=["leverage3"]),
  st("November 2025", "Twenty-nine banks in ten countries", WORLD, 1.15, "Twenty-Nine Banks Too Large to Be Allowed to Fail",
   "Each November the Financial Stability Board, which also meets in Basel, publishes a list. It names the banks so large and so tied to each other that the failure of one would damage the whole world's finances. The official term is global systemically important banks. The 2025 list has 29, the same banks as the year before. Eight are American, five Chinese, four French, three Japanese, three British and two Canadian, with one each from Germany, Switzerland, the Netherlands and Spain. JPMorgan Chase stands alone in the highest category. Every one of the 29 is on the map. Being listed means holding a little more capital. It also tells every lender in the world which banks governments will not let collapse. A bank on that list can borrow more cheaply than a bank that might be allowed to die, because lending to it is safer. So the list written to contain the danger also pays the banks that create it. Part 3 puts a figure on that payment.",
   "https://www.fsb.org/2025/11/2025-list-of-global-systemically-important-banks-g-sibs/", w=1,
   add={"United States": 0, "China": 0, "France": 0, "Japan": 0, "United Kingdom": 0, "Canada": 0, "Germany": 0, "Switzerland": 0, "Netherlands": 0, "Spain": 0},
   pl=["fsb", "jpm", "citi", "bofa", "gs", "ms", "wfc", "bny", "statestreet", "icbc", "abc", "boc", "ccb", "bocom", "bnp", "ca", "bpce", "socgen", "mufg", "smfg", "mizuho", "hsbc", "barclays", "stanchart", "rbc", "td", "deutsche", "ubs", "ing", "santander"],
   ex=["gsib2025"], note="Every bank on the Financial Stability Board's 2025 list is drawn as a ringed circle at its headquarters, and the ten home countries are shaded. Nothing is left out."),
 ])

flow(id="imf", part=1, scope="world",
 title="The Fund: The Lender a Government Cannot Refuse",
 kick="When a country runs out of dollars it turns to the International Monetary Fund, where votes are counted by wealth.",
 sum="The swap lines in an earlier chapter reached fourteen central banks. A country outside that circle that runs short of dollars has one main door to knock on: the International Monetary Fund in Washington. The Fund lends foreign currency to governments that can no longer pay for imports or foreign debts. Private lenders usually wait for the Fund's approval before they lend again, so its decision opens or shuts every other door as well.<br><br>That makes the Fund's own rules a matter of the first importance to billions of people. It is not run on the basis of one country, one vote. Votes follow the money each member has paid in, which follows the size of its economy. This short chapter sets out who holds the votes and how a loan is paid out, because Part 6 will return to the countries that owe.",
 bridge="The first slide shows how votes are shared and why one member can block any major change. The second explains how a loan from the Fund is released in stages, and what that gives the lender.",
 out="At the Fund, the United States alone holds enough votes to block any major decision, and loans are released in stages as a government meets agreed targets. The next chapter looks at three private firms whose grades decide what every borrower pays.",
 rec="The lender of last resort for governments counts votes by wealth, and one member alone can block any change to its rules.",
 roles=["Holds enough votes to block major decisions alone"],
 src=[{"t": "Congressional Research Service, The International Monetary Fund (In Focus IF10676, December 2025)", "u": "https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/2025-12-08_IF10676_7a228a2f7260ce52002fe292eff912f700492288.html"}],
 steps=[
  st("2025", "Washington", WASH, 2.2, "Votes by Wealth, and One Veto",
   "Nearly every country belongs to the International Monetary Fund. Each pays in a sum called a quota, set mainly by the size of its economy, and its share of the votes follows its quota. The research service of the United States Congress sets out the result. Major decisions at the Fund need 85 per cent of the votes. The United States holds 16.49 per cent, the most of any member. Since 100 minus 16.49 is less than 85, nothing major can pass against its wishes. No other country has that power alone. Ordinary decisions, such as approving a loan to a particular country, need a simple majority, and the rich countries together hold one. Think about what this means for a borrower. The body that decides whether its government receives emergency dollars, and on what terms, is steered by the governments whose banks and investors are owed the money. The borrower's own vote is a fraction of one per cent. The rules cannot be changed without the consent of the member that gains most from them.",
   "https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/2025-12-08_IF10676_7a228a2f7260ce52002fe292eff912f700492288.html", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["imf", "worldbank"], ex=["imf_votes"]),
  st("Today", "Washington", WASH, 2.2, "A Loan Paid Out in Stages",
   "A loan from the Fund is called a programme, and the money does not arrive all at once. It is paid in instalments over two or three years. The Fund's board, voting by simple majority, approves the programme and then reviews it. Each instalment is released when the review finds that the government has kept to the plan agreed at the start. That structure is what gives the lender its hold. A government that has run out of dollars needs every instalment, so the plan becomes the country's economic policy for as long as the programme lasts, whatever its voters were promised at the last election. Recall the sequence from the mechanics chapters. A rise in the American interest rate can push a country into exactly this position through no decision of its own. The chain then runs from a committee in Washington that sets the rate to a board in Washington that sets the terms of the rescue. Part 6 maps the countries now carrying the heaviest foreign debts.",
   "https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/2025-12-08_IF10676_7a228a2f7260ce52002fe292eff912f700492288.html", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["imf"],
   note="Shaded: the United States, where the Fund has its headquarters and which holds the largest vote. The countries that borrow are mapped in Part 6."),
 ])

flow(id="ratings", part=1, scope="world",
 title="The Rating Agencies: Three Firms Grade the World",
 kick="Three private companies decide how risky every large borrower is, and they are paid by the borrowers they grade.",
 sum="A lender wants to know whether it will be repaid. For bonds, that judgement has been handed to credit rating agencies, which grade each borrower and each bond on a scale from AAA, the safest, down to D, which means it has stopped paying. The grade matters a great deal. Many pension funds and insurers are only allowed to hold bonds above a certain grade, so a lower grade shrinks the pool of lenders and raises the interest the borrower must pay.<br><br>The grading is done by companies, and three of them do nearly all of it. They grade governments as well as firms. They are paid by the borrower whose bond they are grading. This chapter shows how concentrated the business is, what happened when the sellers of the bonds were paying for the grades, and what a grade costs a poor country.",
 bridge="The first slide measures the three firms' hold on the business and explains who pays them. The second shows the result in the years before 2008. The third shows the difference in interest between a well-graded government and a poorly graded one.",
 out="Three firms hold about nine-tenths of the market for credit ratings, they are paid by the issuers, and an official inquiry called their failures essential to the crash of 2008. Their grades help decide why Africa pays 9.8 per cent and the United States 2.8. That completes the top of the system. Part 3 goes into the banks.",
 rec="Three firms paid by the borrowers they grade decide what borrowing costs for companies and for whole countries.",
 roles=["Home of the three large rating agencies"],
 src=[{"t": "Congressional Research Service, Credit Rating Agencies: Background and Regulatory Issues (In Focus IF11916, April 2024)", "u": "https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IF11916.html"},
      {"t": "Financial Crisis Inquiry Commission, Conclusions (2011)", "u": "https://fcic.law.stanford.edu/report/conclusions"},
      {"t": "UN Trade and Development, Developing countries face record-high public debt burdens (26 June 2025)", "u": "https://unctad.org/news/developing-countries-face-record-high-public-debt-burdens-now-time-reform"}],
 steps=[
  st("2024", "New York", NY, 3.2, "Three Firms, Nine-Tenths of the Market",
   "The United States registers the rating agencies whose grades may be used in its financial rules. In March 2024 there were ten. The research service of Congress reports that three of them, which the regulator classes as large, account for 91 per cent of the market. They are S&P Global Ratings, Moody's and Fitch. Two things make their position hard to shake. First, the regulator began writing ratings into its rules in 1975, so a grade from a recognised agency became something a borrower had to have. Second, the agencies are usually paid by the issuer. That means the company or government selling the bond pays for the grade on it. Consider the position this creates. The seller chooses which agency to hire. An agency that grades harshly can lose the customer to one that does not. The buyer of the bond, whose money is at risk, pays nothing and has no say. The next slide shows what that arrangement produced when it was tested.",
   "https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IF11916.html", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["sp", "moodys", "fitch", "sec"], ex=["ratings91"]),
  st("2000 to 2008", "New York", NY, 3.2, "Paid by the Seller: The Grades Behind 2008",
   "In the years before 2008, banks bundled thousands of home loans together and sold slices of the bundles as bonds. This is called securitisation. The banks needed top grades to sell them, and they paid the agencies for each grading. The work became the agencies' main business. By the first quarter of 2007, grading these bundled products brought in 53 per cent of Moody's revenue, according to reporting cited by the research service of Congress. Enormous volumes of bonds built on risky home loans were given the highest grade, AAA, the same as the safest governments. When borrowers stopped paying, the bonds collapsed in value, and pension funds and banks around the world that had trusted the grade took the losses. The commission that investigated for Congress concluded that the failures of the credit rating agencies were essential cogs in the wheel of financial destruction. The three firms kept their fees. They remain the three firms, with the same way of being paid.",
   "https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IF11916.html", add={"United States": 0}, pl=["moodys", "sp", "fitch", "fcic"], ex=["fcic2011", "ratings91"]),
  st("2020 to 2025", "The whole world", WORLD, 1.15, "A Grade Sets a Country's Interest Bill",
   "The same three firms grade governments. A country with a low grade is treated as a risky borrower, fewer funds are allowed to hold its bonds, and it has to offer more interest to sell them. UN Trade and Development reports the outcome. Since 2020, developing regions have borrowed at rates two to four times higher than the United States. Go back to the figures from the mechanics: 2.8 per cent for the United States, 9.8 per cent for the average African government. A grade is an opinion about the future, formed in an office in New York or London. Yet it works like a tax. Every point of extra interest is money that leaves a poor country's budget each year and goes to bondholders abroad. And the opinion feeds itself. A lower grade raises the interest bill, a higher interest bill makes the debt harder to carry, and a debt that is harder to carry earns a lower grade. Part 6 maps the countries caught in that loop.",
   "https://unctad.org/news/developing-countries-face-record-high-public-debt-burdens-now-time-reform", w=1, pl=["unctad", "sp", "moodys", "fitch"], ex=["unctad_rates"],
   note="This slide shows the whole world and shades no country, because the source compares regions. The ringed circles are the three rating agencies and the UN body that published the comparison."),
 ])
